<p>Let&#x27;s move on to configuring GitHub Workflows for continuous deployments.</p>
<h2 id="configure-github-actions-for-continuous-deployment-cd-workflow" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Configure GitHub Actions for Continuous Deployment (CD) Workflow</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#configure-github-actions-for-continuous-deployment-cd-workflow" aria-label="Link to Configure GitHub Actions for Continuous Deployment (CD) Workflow"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>First, let&#x27;s add all the required environment variables obtained in the AWS steps above to your GitHub repo as repository Secrets. Go to your GitHub repository&#x27;s Settings, and click on <strong>Secrets and Variables</strong>. Then, click on <strong>New repository secret</strong>.</p>
